transcripts are not polyadenylated. We therefore developed total RNA‐associated protein purification (TRAPP) based on 254\xa0nm UV crosslinking and purification of all RNA–protein complexes using silica beads. In a variant approach (PAR‐TRAPP), RNAs were labelled with 4‐thiouracil prior to 350\xa0nm crosslinking. PAR‐TRAPP in yeast identified hundreds of RNA binding proteins, strongly enriched for canonical RBPs. In comparison, TRAPP identified many more proteins not expected to bind RNA, and this correlated strongly with protein abundance. Comparing TRAPP in yeast and E.\xa0coli showed apparent conservation of RNA binding by metabolic enzymes. Illustrating the value of total RBP purification, we discovered that the glycolytic enzyme enolase interacts with tRNAs. Exploiting PAR‐TRAPP to determine the effects of brief exposure to weak acid stress revealed specific changes in late 60S ribosome biogenesis. Furthermore, we identified the precise sites of crosslinking for hundreds of RNA–peptide conjugates, using iTRAPP, providing insights into potential regulation. We conclude that TRAPP is a widely applicable tool for RBPome characterization.\n\n[METHODS]\nMaterials and Methods\nReagents and Tools table\nMethods and Protocols\nYeast SILAC media and crosslinking\nYeast cells (BY4741 derived MATa, his3∆1 leu2∆0 met15∆0 ura3∆0, arg4Δ, Lys9Δ) were grown at 30°C under shaking in yeast nitrogen base media (Formedium CYN0410), containing 2% w/v of D‐Glucose (Fisher G/0500/60), supplemented with Complete Supplement Mixture without tryptophan, lysine, arginine and uracil (Formedium DCS1339) and with 20\xa0mg\xa0l−1 uracil (Sigma U0750‐100g). SILAC light media were additionally supplemented with 30\xa0mg\xa0l−1 lysine (Sigma L5626‐100g) and 5\xa0mg\xa0l−1 arginine (A5131‐100g), while 30\xa0mg\xa0l−1 13C6 lysine (Cambridge Isotope Laboratories CLM‐2247‐H‐0.25) and 5\xa0mg\xa0l−1 13C6 arginine (Cambridge Isotope Laboratories CLM‐2265‐0.5) were added to SILAC heavy media. Equal cell mass (by OD measurement) of heavy‐labelled and equal cell mass of light‐labelled cells were combined per experiment.\nFor TRAPP using UVC (254\xa0nm) crosslinking, the culture was harvested at OD600 0.5 from 750\xa0ml of media and irradiated with Megatron apparatus (UVO3) as described (Granneman et\xa0al, 2011). Cells were harvested by filtration and immediately frozen for later processing.\nFor PAR‐TRAPP, using UVA (350\xa0nm) crosslinking, cells were grown to OD 0.15, 4‐thiouracil (4tU) (Sigma 440736‐1g) was added to 0.5\xa0mM from 1\xa0M DMSO stock solution, and cells were allowed to grow for 3\xa0h. Cells were harvested by filtration and resuspended in 800\xa0ml of growth media without 4tU and placed on the irradiation tray of the eBox\xa0irradiation apparatus. The UVA lamps were pre‐warmed for 1\xa0min, and the cells were irradiated for 38\xa0s delivering 7.33\xa0J\xa0cm−2 of UVA. Cells were harvested by filtration and frozen for later processing.\nEqual cell mass of heavy‐labelled and equal cell mass of light‐labelled cells were combined per experiment before lysis step.\n E.\xa0coli SILAC media and crosslinking\n Escherichia coli cells (MG1655 derived F‐, Lambda‐, rph‐1 ΔlysA ΔargA) (Starosta et\xa0al, 2014) were grown in 150\xa0ml of M9 minimal medium, supplemented with 1\xa0μM FeNH4SO4, 0.2% w/v of D‐Glucose (Fisher G/0500/60), 1\xa0mM MgSO4. SILAC light media were additionally supplemented with 40\xa0mg\xa0l−1 lysine (Sigma L5626‐100g) and 40\xa0mg\xa0l−1 arginine (A5131‐100g), while 40\xa0mg\xa0l−1 [13C6]‐lysine (Cambridge Isotope Laboratories CLM‐2247‐H‐0.25) and 40\xa0mg\xa0l−1 [13C6]‐arginine (Cambridge Isotope Laboratories CLM‐2265‐0.5) were added to SILAC heavy media. Cells were grown to OD 0.5, diluted to 700\xa0ml with growth media and irradiated with UVC (254\xa0nm) using a Minitron (UVO3) (Granneman et\xa0al, 2011). Equal cell mass (70 ODs) of heavy‐labelled and equal cell mass of light‐labelled cells were combined per experiment before lysis step.\nSorbic acid treatment\nYeast cells were treated as described above with 4tU in SILAC yeast nitrogen base media for 3\xa0h. Sorbic acid was added to 6\xa0mM final concentration from ethanol stock solution, and cells were incubated for 16\xa0min. Cells were harvested and irradiated as described above.\neBOX irradiation apparatus\nThe eBox is a chamber with internal dimensions of 49.5\xa0×\xa025.5\xa0×\xa048\xa0cm (W\xa0×\xa0H\xa0×\xa0D) lined with aluminium foil. The sample tray made of borosilicate glass is positioned between 2 banks of 10 PL‐L 36W/09/4P PUVA 350‐nm bulbs (Philips 871150061410040). Each bank is driven by 5 PC236TCLPRO‐TR ballasts (Tridonic 22176170). The two banks together consume 720\xa0W of electrical power. UVA measurements were performed with Sper Scientific UVA/B Light Meter (850009), equipped with UVFS Reflective ND Filter, OD: 2.0 (THORLABS NDUV20B). Based on the spectral sensitivity of the meter, we estimate that in 38\xa0s of irradiation time, we administer from 7.3\xa0J\xa0cm−2 of UVA (193\xa0mW\xa0cm−2). The UVA output of the eBox\xa0stabilizes after 1\xa0min of lamp warmup. The sample is prevented from UVA exposure with sliding shutters during the warmup period, followed by shutters’ extraction and sample irradiation for 38\xa0s.\nSilica bead preparation\nSilica beads (Fluka S5631‐500g) were resuspended in 1\xa0M HCl and left for 24\xa0h at room temperature. The beads were then washed with water three times by spinning in a 50‐ml falcon tube at 3,000 g for 2\xa0min and decanting the supernatant, containing beads of the smallest size. After the last wash, beads are resuspended in volume of water to achieve 50% slurry suspension.\nCell lysis\n Escherichia coli cells pellets (140 ODs) were resuspended in 8\xa0ml of 1:1 mix between phenol (Sigma P4557), saturated with 10\xa0mM Tris–HCl pH 8.0, 1\xa0mM EDTA and GTC buffer (4\xa0M guanidine thiocyanate (Fisher BP221‐1), 50\xa0mM Tris–HCl pH 8.0 (Invitrogen\xa015504‐020), 10\xa0mM EDTA (Fisher D/0700/53), 1% beta‐mercaptoethanol), the sample was split between 8 2‐ml screwcap tubes, 1 volume of zirconia beads (BioSpec 11079105z) was added, and cells were lysed with FastPrep‐24 5\xa0g (MP biomedicals 116005500) twice for 40\xa0s at 6\xa0m\xa0s−1. Lysates were recovered into 50‐ml falcon tube.\nYeast mixed cell pellets were resuspended in 2\xa0ml of phenol‐GTC buffer in a 50‐ml falcon tube, 3\xa0ml of zirconia beads was added, and cells were vortexed for 1\xa0min 6 times, with 1‐min incubation on ice between vortexing. 6\xa0ml of phenol‐GTC mix was added, and lysates were vortexed for additional 1\xa0min.\nRNP isolation\nSpin down samples in 50‐ml falcon tubes at 4,600 g, 4°C for 5\xa0min.\nRecover the supernatant into 2‐ml tubes and spin at 13,000 g for 10\xa0min to clear the lysate.\nRecover the cleared lysate into a 50‐ml falcon tube; avoid taking any pellet at this step.\nTo the recovered lysate, add 0.1 volume of 3 M sodium acetate‐acetic acid pH 4.0, mix briefly and slowly add 1 volume of absolute ethanol, vortex to mix.\nAdd 1\xa0ml of 50% slurry suspension of silica in water (per 400 ODs of yeast cells), followed by 0.5\xa0ml of ethanol to maintain 50% ethanol concentration in the mix.\nLoad nucleic acids onto silica for 60\xa0min on a rotating wheel at room temperature.\nSpin down the beads at 2,500 g, 4°C for 2\xa0min and resuspend the beads by vigorous vortexing in 15\xa0ml of wash buffer I (4 M guanidine thiocyanate, 1\xa0M sodium acetate (Alfa Aesar 11554)–acetic acid (Fisher A/0400/PB17) pH 4.0, 30% ethanol). At this point, samples can tolerate harsh mixing; therefore, the use of a FastPrep or a similar homogenizer with the corresponding 50\xa0ml adapter is possible.\nPellet the resuspended silica at 2,500 g, 4°C for 2\xa0min, and discard the supernatant. One can use a vacuum system or pipette to remove as much of wash buffer as possible. Repeat the wash with buffer I 2 more times.\nWash the beads three times with 20\xa0ml of wash buffer II (100\xa0mM NaCl, 50\xa0mM Tris–HCl pH 6.4, 80% ethanol) using the procedure described for wash buffer I.\nAfter the third wash, resuspend the beads in a small amount of wash buffer II using a vortex or a homogenizer. Transfer the resuspended silica to two 2‐ml tubes.\nSpin the tubes at 2,000 g for 2\xa0min at room temperature and discard the supernatant.\nDry the silica for 10\xa0min in a SpeedVac at 45°C.\nElute the nucleic acids three times with 500\xa0μl per 2‐ml tube. Use vortex or a homogenizer to resuspend the silica and spin at 4,000\xa0g for 1\xa0min to pellet the silica. Recover the supernatant into two new 2‐ml tubes. As the dissolved nucleic acids make the recovered supernatant viscous, some silica beads will be recovered together with the supernatant.\nSpin the recovered 2\xa0×\xa01.5\xa0ml supernatant at 14,000 g for 1\xa0min at room temperature. Transfer the supernatant into 2 fresh 2‐ml tubes and repeat the spin.\nRecover the supernatant into two 2‐ml protein LoBind tubes (Eppendorf 0030108132). Add 0.5\xa0μl of RNaseA+T1 (Ambion AM2286) mix per tube and incubate the samples for 2\xa0h in a SpeedVac at 45°C, followed by SpeedVac incubation at 65°C until the samples are fully dry. Alternatively, the sample can be left overnight in a SpeedVac at 45°C, which takes approximately 16\xa0h to dry. If the sample is expected to have a large amount of DNA, consider adding a DNAse step or using a promiscuous nuclease such as benzonase or cyanase.\nThe dried samples are resuspended in 75\xa0μl Laemmli buffer per 2‐ml tube by vortexing, incubated at 100°C for 10\xa0min. At this point, the two 2‐ml tubes are merged into one.\nResolve approximately 10\xa0μl on a gradient 4–20% Mini‐PROTEAN TGX gel (Biorad 4561095), and stain with Imperial protein stain (Thermo 24615) according to the manufacturer\'s protocol. The amount of sample to be loaded on a gel depends on the concentration of proteins in the sample. A good strategy is to run a test gel with a few varying loading to determine the optimal amount of sample to process.\nEach lane of the gel, corresponding to one SILAC mi\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nSaccharomyces cerevisiae (baker\'s yeast); Escherichia coli\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
